      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2016 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "STUDY OBJECTIVE",
            "content": "To explore how pushing during labor and body mass index affect the development of postdural puncture headache in parturients who experienced dural puncture with Tuohy needles."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "DESIGN",
            "content": "Retrospective cohort."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "SETTING",
            "content": "Obstetric ward and operating rooms at a university-affiliated hospital."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "PATIENTS",
            "content": "One hundred ninety parturients who had witnessed dural puncture with 17 or 18 gauge Tuohy needles from 1999-2014."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
            "content": "Patients were categorized by pushing status and body mass index (kg/m(2)): nonobese <30, obese 30-39.99, morbidly obese 40-49.99, and super obese ≥50."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "MEASUREMENTS",
            "content": "Headache, number of days of headache, maximum headache score, and epidural blood patch placement."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "MAIN RESULTS",
            "content": "Compared with women who did not push, women who pushed during labor had increased risk of postdural puncture headache (odds ratio [OR], 2.1 [1.1-4.0]; P=.02), more days of headache (P=.02), and increased epidural blood patch placement (P=.02). Super obese patients were less likely to develop headache compared with nonobese (OR, 0.33 [0.13-0.85]; P=.02), obese (OR, 0.37 [0.14-0.98]; P=.045], and morbidly obese patients (OR, 0.20 [0.05-0.68]; P<.01). In a multivariate logistic regression model, lack of pushing (OR, 0.57 [0.29-1.10]; P=.096) and super obesity (OR, 0.41 [0.16-1.02]; P=.056] were no longer significantly associated with reduced risk of postdural puncture headache."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Parturients who did not push before delivery and parturients with body mass index ≥50kg/m(2) were less likely to develop postdural puncture headache in a univariate analysis. Similar trends were demonstrated in a multivariate model, but were no longer statistically significant."
          }
        ]
      },
